# DeckBorne — central configuration
# Sourced by install.sh and every script in scripts/. Edit values here, not inline.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- DeckBorne itself -------------------------------------------------------
# Bumped at release, and matched against the tag /releases/latest redirects to.
# An install that does not carry this cannot be identified by a later updater.
DECKBORNE_VERSION="${DECKBORNE_VERSION:-0.8.0}"
DECKBORNE_REPO="${DECKBORNE_REPO:-JimminyRiggit/DeckBorne}"

# --- shadPS4 emulator (pinned release) --------------------------------------
# Pinned to the official v0.16.0 Linux SDL build. The SDL build boots straight
# into a game from a CLI path, which is what the Steam tile needs (no GUI browser).
SHADPS4_VERSION="v.0.16.0"
SHADPS4_ZIP_URL="https://github.com/shadps4-emu/shadPS4/releases/download/v.0.16.0/shadps4-linux-sdl-0.16.0.zip"
# Verified 2026-07-15 by downloading the asset:
SHADPS4_ZIP_SHA256="7cbb19fe8c909e04129d2431eef723d4710499b40d1aed0047681d14a1dfc79b"
SHADPS4_APPIMAGE_NAME="Shadps4-sdl.AppImage"
SHADPS4_APPIMAGE_SHA256="9c3656caec6d2e96e9b54ae112c28f4a16ad28780ad9d36affa2ded9ba8fba1a"

# --- PKG extractor ----------------------------------------------------------
# shadPS4 REMOVED its built-in PKG installer; the SDL build can only launch an
# already-extracted game. So we extract the .pkg with the ShadPs4Plus standalone
# extractor (built from shadPS4's own extraction code → natively compatible).
# v1.1 is REQUIRED: it fixes file corruption when extracting PKGs over 2GB
# (our base game is 30GB). Bundled at payloads/shadps4/ for offline installs.
PKG_EXTRACTOR_APPIMAGE="ShadPs4Plus-PkgExtractor-1.1-linux.AppImage"
PKG_EXTRACTOR_URL="https://github.com/AzaharPlus/shadPS4Plus/releases/download/PKG_EXTRACTOR_1_1/ShadPs4Plus-PkgExtractor-1.1-linux.AppImage"
PKG_EXTRACTOR_SHA256="b360bc691aa3d89071aa707224155f6eade348d314cf7798ac550e31c11a7bcc"  # verified 2026-07-15

# --- game dump (user-supplied, already present in game-pkg/) -----------------
GAME_NAME="Bloodborne"
# The base/update .pkg and the title-id are now DISCOVERED from the PKG headers at
# install time (any release/region naming works — see discover_* in lib.sh). This is
# only a FALLBACK title-id, used if a dump's header can't be read.
GAME_TITLE_ID="CUSA03173"          # EU GOTY / Complete Edition (incl. The Old Hunters DLC)
# Optional OVERRIDES: set these globs to force a specific .pkg (relative to repo root);
# leave them and discovery picks the largest valid .pkg as base + its same-title patch.
GAME_BASE_PKG_GLOB=""
GAME_UPDATE_PKG_GLOB=""
# Save data is filed under a DIFFERENT title-id than the disc (confirmed on-device
# 2026-07-28: an EU GOTY dump, CUSA03173, saves under CUSA00207/SPRJ0005).
GAME_SAVE_TITLE_ID="${GAME_SAVE_TITLE_ID:-CUSA00207}"
GAME_SAVE_DIR_GLOB="${GAME_SAVE_DIR_GLOB:-SPRJ*}"

# --- install destinations on the Steam Deck --------------------------------
# DeckBorne's own small state (currently just the remembered install location). Under
# $HOME on purpose — it must be readable when the SD card is out, since it is what tells
# an uninstall where the game went.
DECKBORNE_STATE_DIR="${DECKBORNE_STATE_DIR:-${HOME}/.local/share/DeckBorne}"
DECKBORNE_STORAGE_FILE="${DECKBORNE_STORAGE_FILE:-${DECKBORNE_STATE_DIR}/storage_root}"

# WHERE THE ~30GB GOES. Default $HOME (the internal drive) — which makes GAMES_DIR
# byte-identical to every install predating this setting, so nothing migrates.
# Set it to a mounted device root to install onto an SD card or USB drive, e.g.
#     DECKBORNE_STORAGE_ROOT=/run/media/mmcblk0p1 ./install.sh
# The UI writes it here after the user picks a device; scripts/detect_storage.py lists
# the candidates and validates one (stage 00 calls it with --check).
#
# Resolution order, and the order matters: an explicit env var beats the remembered
# choice, so a single-stage test run can target somewhere else without disturbing what
# the last full install recorded.
#   1. DECKBORNE_STORAGE_ROOT from the environment
#   2. $DECKBORNE_STORAGE_FILE — written by stage 00 once a location is validated
#   3. $HOME
# ⚠ A REMEMBERED ROOT IS NOT CHECKED FOR EXISTENCE HERE, deliberately. If the SD card is
# out, the right behaviour is to keep pointing at it — so the uninstall still removes the
# game once it is back, and so stage 00 can fail with "is it still plugged in?" instead
# of silently falling back to $HOME and re-extracting 30GB to the wrong device.
if [ -z "${DECKBORNE_STORAGE_ROOT:-}" ] && [ -r "$DECKBORNE_STORAGE_FILE" ]; then
  DECKBORNE_STORAGE_ROOT="$(head -n1 "$DECKBORNE_STORAGE_FILE" 2>/dev/null || true)"
fi
DECKBORNE_STORAGE_ROOT="${DECKBORNE_STORAGE_ROOT:-$HOME}"

# ⚠ APP_DIR STAYS IN $HOME EVEN WHEN THE GAME DOES NOT, and this is load-bearing, not
# laziness. The Steam tile's Exe is "$APP_DIR/$SHADPS4_APPIMAGE_NAME", and grid_appid()
# HASHES THE QUOTED Exe (see CLAUDE.md, Steam fact 6). Moving APP_DIR would change the
# appid — so a tile created while the card was in would become invisible to the
# uninstaller's --by-exe match the moment the storage choice changed, stranding it in the
# library forever. It is also ~100MB against the game's 30GB, so there is nothing to gain.
# Keeping it in $HOME additionally means the emulator is still present with the card out.
APP_DIR="${HOME}/Applications/shadps4"          # emulator AppImage lives here
# ⚠ MUST stay in sync with GAMES_SUBPATH in scripts/detect_storage.py — that is what the
# UI reports free space against and what it checks for an existing install.
GAMES_DIR="${DECKBORNE_STORAGE_ROOT}/Games/shadps4"   # extracted games (CUSAxxxxx/eboot.bin)

# ⚠ shadPS4's user directory is XDG_DATA_HOME, NOT ~/.config. Verified in
# src/common/path_util.cpp at tag v.0.16.0: the Linux branch builds
# `$HOME/.local/share/shadPS4` (or $XDG_DATA_HOME/shadPS4). The string
# `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` does not appear ANYWHERE in the source, at 0.15 or 0.16.
# Note the case: shadPS4, capital P S and 4.
#
# We wrote ~/.config/shadps4/config.toml for weeks. Nothing ever read it — every
# setting the emulator reported was simply its built-in default. Corroborated on-device:
# the file said isDevKitMode=true while the boot log said `isDevKit: false`.
SHADPS4_USER_DIR="${SHADPS4_USER_DIR:-${HOME}/.local/share/shadPS4}"

# ⚠ 0.16 dropped TOML for JSON. src/common/config.cpp is GONE at v.0.16.0; settings moved
# to src/core/emulator_settings.cpp and the file is config.json. Keys are the C++ member
# names verbatim (NLOHMANN_DEFINE_TYPE_NON_INTRUSIVE), so they are snake_case:
# `vblank_frequency` not `vblankFrequency`, `dev_kit_mode` not `isDevKit`,
# `full_screen` not `Fullscreen`.
#
# ⚠ NEVER write a config.toml into SHADPS4_USER_DIR. If a .toml is present there and
# config.json is not, 0.16 pops a BLOCKING SDL_ShowMessageBox migration prompt — which
# during the headless warm-up would hang the install with no visible window.
SHADPS4_CONFIG_JSON="${SHADPS4_CONFIG_JSON:-${SHADPS4_USER_DIR}/config.json}"

# Legacy path. Nothing reads it; kept so uninstall still cleans up what old runs wrote.
CONFIG_DIR="${HOME}/.config/shadps4"            # DEAD — pre-0.16 config.toml location
MODS_STAGE_DIR="${HOME}/Games/shadps4-mods"     # staged mod files before merge

# --- game patches (shadPS4's own patch system) -------------------------------
# NOT "mods". shadPS4 applies memory patches to the running game from XML files it
# reads at boot. This is where 60 FPS / QOL live for Bloodborne, and it is entirely
# separate from the file-overlay mods in payloads/mods/.
#
# WHY WE FETCH INSTEAD OF BUNDLING: the upstream patch repos declare NO license, so
# DeckBorne must not redistribute their XML. Fetching at install time also keeps the
# shipped artifact small and always current. It costs one small HTTP GET.
#
# The Qt GUI that used to download these was REMOVED from the emulator (Qt dropped
# ~0.16.x); it now lives in the separate shadps4-qtlauncher repo. The SDL build we
# ship has no downloader — but the core still auto-applies patches at boot, so
# writing the files ourselves is the supported path, not a hack.
PATCHES_URL="${PATCHES_URL:-https://raw.githubusercontent.com/shadps4-emu/ps4_cheats/main/PATCHES/Bloodborne.xml}"
PATCHES_XML_NAME="Bloodborne.xml"
# The emulator iterates EVERY subdirectory of patches/ and reads its files.json.
# Named after the source repo, matching the Qt launcher's convention, so a user who
# later runs that launcher updates this file rather than creating a rival copy.
PATCHES_SOURCE_DIR="shadPS4"
# Derived from SHADPS4_USER_DIR so the patches and config can never drift apart.
PATCHES_DIR="${PATCHES_DIR:-${SHADPS4_USER_DIR}/patches}"

# Patches enabled per profile, ';'-separated. Names must match the `Name=` attribute
# in the XML EXACTLY — stage 35 fails loudly on a name it can't find rather than
# quietly installing nothing.
#
# Both profiles get the Deck light-grid patch. Author's note: "Increases performance by
#   lowering Light grid draw calls. USE VERSION THAT MATCHES YOUR WINDOW/FULLSCREEN
#   RESOLUTION, NOT RENDER RESOLUTION." Pure perf, no gameplay change, so it is
#   vanilla-safe — and 1280x800 is the Deck's native panel.
#   ⚠ RESOLUTION-KEYED: there are 1080p / 1440p / 4k variants too. Playing DOCKED to an
#   external display changes the window resolution, and the 1280x800 variant is then the
#   wrong one. Handheld is DeckBorne's target, so 1280x800 is the right default — revisit
#   if docked play ever becomes a supported mode.
#   ⚠ Upstream also notes "1080p light grid is also in the performance patch" — so if the
#   deckborne profile ever adds "Performance Patch", check for overlap first.
# vanilla — PROMOTED FROM CHOCOLATE 2026-07-19, after the artifacting test resolved. This
#   is the shipping default: chocolate's proven 10-patch set (since trimmed), no frame-rate patch,
#   artifact-free on-device. The matching settings (Fifo, FSR, extra_dmem) came across too
#   — see the per-profile settings block further down.
#   ⚠ TRIMMED IN TWO ROUNDS to the current 7 patches. 2026-07-19 dropped "Skip Intro" and
#   "Disable Motion Blur (perf increase)"; 2026-07-20 dropped "Disable Chromatic
#   Aberration". All three are presentation choices, not Deck-compatibility fixes, and the
#   promotion had swept them in from chocolate without anyone deciding vanilla wanted them.
#   All three remain in deckborne.
#   ⚠ SUBSET OF A PROVEN SET, NOT ITSELF RE-TESTED. The artifact-free on-device run was the
#   10-patch version. Dropping patches cannot introduce the artifacting (that was FPS++,
#   still absent), so this is low-risk — but "Disable Motion Blur" was a perf win as well as
#   a look change, so vanilla is now marginally HEAVIER than the set that was measured.
#   If frame pacing looks worse than the 2026-07-19 run, this trim is the variable.
#   ⚠ STILL NOT LITERALLY "stock": it keeps Model LOD 1 (Lower) and FSR upscaling.
#   Describe it as "stock feel, Deck-tuned", never "unmodified".
#   ⚠ "30 FPS++" IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT AND MUST STAY THAT WAY. It is the CONFIRMED cause
#   of the vertex-explosion artifacting (single-variable run 2026-07-19). Fixing it requires
#   a Nexus MOD we are not allowed to bundle, so any frame-rate patch here would ship a
#   guaranteed visual bug to every user. Frame-rate work belongs in deckborne, gated.
#   NB that run also EXONERATES "Model LOD 1 (Lower)": it was enabled in both the
#   artifacting and the clean state, so it cannot be the cause. No separate run needed.
#   COST, accepted knowingly: with no FPS++ patch the game runs stock frame pacing (30
#   target, via vblank 60 and Bloodborne's divide-by-2 flip rate) and loses the input-delay
#   and frame-skip work that "30 FPS++" exists to provide. Per its author it "changes some
#   frame settings like the frame skip, vsync, frame tearing" to improve "input
#   delay/response times for 30 FPS" — a responsiveness fix, not a gameplay change. We give
#   that up in exchange for shipping without a mod dependency.
# deckborne — 60 FPS + community mods, and currently BLOCKED ON A MOD WE CANNOT SHIP. The
#   frame-rate patches are only usable alongside the user-supplied vertex-explosion fix, so
#   deckborne cannot ship ungated: without the mod it artifacts immediately. The mod is NOT
#   yet in config/mods.catalog and the "FPS++ + mod = clean" chain is UNVERIFIED on this
#   hardware — that test runs on `chocolate`, which is why 30 FPS++ went back there.
#   ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-07-18: this note used to justify the delay by quoting "game speed
#   might slow if you drop below 60 fps" as the 60 FPS patch's tradeoff. That quote is
#   from "60FPS (no deltatime)", NOT from "60 FPS++" — the two had been conflated. The
#   real choice is WHICH FAILURE MODE you want when the Deck dips below 60:
#     60 FPS++             — speed holds through a dip; cloth/corpse physics can 'jump'
#                            (this is the artifact the community calls vertex explosion)
#     60FPS (no deltatime) — physics stay stable; the game runs in SLOW MOTION instead
#   Upstream's note for the ++ family is boilerplate (30/60/90 share it verbatim) and
#   never mentions deltatime, so "++ uses deltatime" is a strong INFERENCE from the other
#   patch being explicitly named "no deltatime" — not a verified fact. Confirm on-device.
#   "Uncap FPS++" is explicitly "not really recommended" upstream — don't reach for it.
#
# NB: names must match the XML's Name= attribute EXACTLY, "(READ NOTES)" suffix included.
# ⚠ NOT INCLUDED: "Performance Patch (perf increase)". It is 226 writes and it CLASHES:
#   - it writes BOTH addresses the 1280x800 Light Grid patch uses (2695cb6, 2695cc0), so
#     it would silently replace our Deck-native light grid with its own (upstream note:
#     "1080p light grid is also in the performance patch")
#   - it shares 5 addresses with 30 FPS++ (2c0118a, 2c011eb, 2c012ca, 2c0137d, 463300)
#   Last-applied wins and the order is the XML's, not ours — so enabling it makes two
#   deliberate choices unpredictable. If you want it, DROP the light grid patch and
#   re-check against 30 FPS++. Verify overlaps by diffing the Address attributes.
#
# The three below were verified to share NO addresses with each other or with the two
# above — 3 writes each, genuinely additive.
#   FMOD Crash Fix        — author's note: "May unintionally prevent some sound playback?"
#                           A stability fix with a possible audio cost. Watch for it.
#   Unlock Game Region    — "Unlocks the game region to support additional language
#                           options, Doesn't switch the X and Circle buttons". NB this
#                           touches LANGUAGE options and this dump is EU (reads
#                           menu/enggb) — if menu text or locale behaviour changes, this
#                           is the first suspect.
#   Disable HTTP Requests — no note; stops the game phoning home.
#   Resolution Patch 1280x800 (16:10) — 82 writes, verified to share NO address with any
#     other enabled patch (nor with the Performance Patch). Author's note: "1280x800
#     resolution with proper lock-on/enemy/ally hp bar coordinates" — it moves the UI to
#     match, not just the render target. Bloodborne renders 1920x1080 on PS4, so this is
#     roughly HALF the pixels, at the Deck's native panel size. Independent of the Light
#     Grid patch: light grid keys off WINDOW resolution, this one off RENDER resolution.
# RECOVERY — the 8-patch set, if only "Disable Chromatic Aberration" needs putting back:
#   1280x800 Light Grid For SteamDeck (READ NOTES);Resolution Patch 1280x800 (16:10);FMOD
#   Crash Fix;Unlock Game Region;Disable HTTP Requests;Disable Chromatic Aberration;
#   Increased Graphics Heap Sizes;Model LOD 1 (Lower)
# RECOVERY — the pre-TRIM vanilla set (the 10 that actually ran on-device), if the trim
#   above needs backing out:
#   1280x800 Light Grid For SteamDeck (READ NOTES);Resolution Patch 1280x800 (16:10);FMOD
#   Crash Fix;Unlock Game Region;Disable HTTP Requests;Skip Intro;Disable Chromatic
#   Aberration;Disable Motion Blur (perf increase);Increased Graphics Heap Sizes;Model LOD
#   1 (Lower)
# RECOVERY — the pre-promotion vanilla set, if this ever needs backing out further:
#   30 FPS++;1280x800 Light Grid For SteamDeck (READ NOTES);Resolution Patch 1280x800
#   (16:10);FMOD Crash Fix;Unlock Game Region;Disable HTTP Requests
PATCHES_VANILLA="${PATCHES_VANILLA:-1280x800 Light Grid For SteamDeck (READ NOTES);Resolution Patch 1280x800 (16:10);FMOD Crash Fix;Unlock Game Region;Disable HTTP Requests;Increased Graphics Heap Sizes;Model LOD 1 (Lower)}"
# PROMOTED FROM CHOCOLATE 2026-07-19, after the vertex-explosion fix was proven on-device.
# This is vanilla's proven 10-patch set PLUS "30 FPS++" — i.e. exactly what chocolate ran
# when the artifacting came back clean. Evidence, from that run's shad_log.txt (NOT from
# config.json — see the "where truth lives" note in CLAUDE.md):
#   * memory_patcher applied "30 FPS++" at 5 offsets — live in memory, not merely requested
#   * stage 40 placed vertex-explosion-fix at dvdroot_ps4/, 144 files replaced
#   * the -UPDATE shadow check never fired, so the modded BASE files are the ones served
#   * 454 /app0/dvdroot_ps4/parts/… opens — the game genuinely read them
#
# ⚠⚠ THIS PROFILE HAS A HARD MOD DEPENDENCY. "30 FPS++" is the CONFIRMED cause of the
# vertex-explosion artifacting; it is only safe here BECAUSE the Nexus vertex-explosion fix
# is applied over it by stage 40. DeckBorne cannot redistribute that mod (see
# config/mods.catalog), so a user who installs this profile with an EMPTY payloads/mods/
# gets 30 FPS++ with no fix and WILL see the artifacting. Stage 40 warns when it finds no
# mods, but nothing currently BLOCKS that combination — see the deckborne notes below.
# ⚠ 2026-07-23: ran "60 FPS++" on-device, then set BACK to "30 FPS++". Two results, and
#   they answer different questions — do not collapse them into "60 didn't work":
#     RENDERING — CLEAN. "60 FPS++" + the vertex-explosion mod shows NO face/cloth
#       explosions. The pairing the notes above called UNVERIFIED is now VERIFIED, and the
#       "FPS++ family needs the mod" rule holds for BOTH members. Log evidence:
#       memory_patcher applied 60 FPS++ at 192 writes (matching the 2026-07-18 count, so
#       the upstream XML is unchanged), dmem 4000, vblank 60, 530 reads under
#       dvdroot_ps4/parts/.
#     PERFORMANCE — NOT FEASIBLE on a standard Deck. That, not artifacting, is why it went
#       back to 30. The hardware cannot hold 60 here; see the Fifo-quantization note below
#       for why a "45 FPS" reading is really 60/30 alternation.
#   So 60 FPS++ is a HARDWARE ceiling, not a correctness problem — which makes it the right
#   basis for a future desktop/handheld-plus profile rather than something to abandon.
#   Swapping the two names is the whole change (vblank stays 60 either way).
# RECOVERY — the pre-promotion deckborne set, if this needs backing out:
#   1280x800 Light Grid For SteamDeck (READ NOTES)
PATCHES_DECKBORNE="${PATCHES_DECKBORNE:-1280x800 Light Grid For SteamDeck (READ NOTES);Resolution Patch 1280x800 (16:10);FMOD Crash Fix;Unlock Game Region;Disable HTTP Requests;Skip Intro;Disable Chromatic Aberration;Disable Motion Blur (perf increase);30 FPS++;Increased Graphics Heap Sizes;Model LOD 1 (Lower)}"

# --- deckborne TARGET: which experience the user picked (2026-07-25) ---------
# deckborne is no longer one config. The UI offers three and this variable carries the
# choice into the pipeline; stage 35 picks the patch string, stage 30 the resolution.
#
#   deck30    30 FPS @ 800p    Steam Deck — recommended   (the historic deckborne config)
#   deck60    60 FPS @ 800p    Steam Deck — beta
#   desktop   60 FPS @ 1080p   desktop-class hardware
#
# ⚠ ONLY THE deckborne PROFILE READS THIS. vanilla and chocolate take their own PATCHES_*
# strings and ignore it — but a TYPO still dies, in install.sh's require_known_target,
# because a silently-ignored bad value is this project's signature bug.
# ⚠ deck30 IS THE DEFAULT and its patch set is byte-identical to what deckborne shipped
# before the choice existed, so any run that does not set this is unchanged.
# ⚠ NOT a fourth profile, deliberately. The stage LIST is identical for all three targets,
# which is what keeps install.sh's `@@DBUI STAGE <idx>` markers aligned with the row list
# in ui/backend.py. A new profile would have to be wired into profile_stages, both stage
# `case`s and the UI's stage arrays; a target only touches the values.
DECKBORNE_TARGET="${DECKBORNE_TARGET:-deck30}"

# deck60 — deckborne's set with the ONE name swapped. Rendering is PROVEN CLEAN on-device
# (2026-07-23, with the vertex-explosion mod applied: 192 writes, no face/cloth explosions),
# and it was reverted to 30 purely because a standard Deck cannot hold 60. Shipping it as an
# opt-in "beta" is that result made available rather than discarded.
# ⚠ vblank stays 60 — the pairing rule gives BOTH ++ variants 60. Do not raise it.
# ⚠ Same hard mod dependency as deck30: the FPS++ family artifacts without the Nexus fix.
PATCHES_DECKBORNE_DECK60="${PATCHES_DECKBORNE_DECK60:-1280x800 Light Grid For SteamDeck (READ NOTES);Resolution Patch 1280x800 (16:10);FMOD Crash Fix;Unlock Game Region;Disable HTTP Requests;Skip Intro;Disable Chromatic Aberration;Disable Motion Blur (perf increase);60 FPS++;Increased Graphics Heap Sizes;Model LOD 1 (Lower)}"

# desktop — 60 FPS at 1080p, for hardware that is not a Deck. Note it is NOT labelled
# "docked": a docked Deck is still a Deck and will not hold 60 at 1080p.
#
# TWO patches differ from deck60, and both are RESOLUTION-KEYED, which is the whole reason
# this cannot be a frame-rate flag:
#   1280x800 Light Grid For SteamDeck  ->  1080p Light Grid (READ NOTES)
#   Resolution Patch 1280x800 (16:10)  ->  Optimal 1080p
#
# ⚠ THERE IS NO "Resolution Patch 1920x1080" UPSTREAM. Verified 2026-07-25 against the live
# XML (59 patches): the 16:9 ladder runs 640x360, 960x540, 1280x720, 1440x810, 1600x900,
# 2560x1440, 3840x2160 — it SKIPS 1080p. `Optimal 1080p` (76 writes) is the only 1080p
# render patch, and it is a DIFFERENT KIND of patch: its note reads "360p global with main
# renders at 1080p", i.e. a 1080p main pass with a cheap global pass, not a flat 1080p
# render. So it is not the exact analogue of the 1280x800 patch, and unlike that one it
# carries no claim about moving the lock-on / HP-bar coordinates to match.
# ⚠ UNVERIFIED ON ANY HARDWARE. Names and additivity are checked (below); how it LOOKS is not.
#
# ⚠ VERIFIED ADDITIVE 2026-07-25 against the live upstream XML — all three deckborne targets
# clash-checked pairwise, NO shared addresses in any of them. Write counts, for comparing
# against memory_patcher's own count in shad_log.txt:
#   deck30   30 FPS++ 97, Resolution Patch 1280x800 82, 1280x800 Light Grid 2
#   deck60   60 FPS++ 192, Resolution Patch 1280x800 82, 1280x800 Light Grid 2
#   desktop  60 FPS++ 192, Optimal 1080p 76, 1080p Light Grid 2
# ⚠ "Performance Patch" STAYS EXCLUDED here too, and the clash is WORSE at 1080p: upstream
# says outright "1080p light grid is also in the performance patch".
#
# ⚠ MODEL LOD IS INVERTED HERE, 2026-07-25 — `Model LOD -2 (Highest)` replaces the Deck's
# `Model LOD 1 (Lower)`. The Deck runs LOWER detail because it has no headroom; the source
# Reddit post ran the HIGHEST end on an RX 6800, which is what this target is for.
# ⚠ THEY ARE THE SAME KNOB — both write ONE address, 0x0216fc09 (verified 2026-07-25). So it
# is a straight swap and NEVER both; enabling two LOD patches makes the result order-dependent.
#
# ⚠ `Disable Motion Blur` and `Disable Chromatic Aberration` STAY, deliberately — they are
# kept as QOL for Bloodborne on PC, not as Deck performance compromises. Do not "restore" them
# on the reasoning that a desktop can afford them; that is not why they are off.
PATCHES_DECKBORNE_DESKTOP="${PATCHES_DECKBORNE_DESKTOP:-1080p Light Grid (READ NOTES);Optimal 1080p;FMOD Crash Fix;Unlock Game Region;Disable HTTP Requests;Skip Intro;Disable Chromatic Aberration;Disable Motion Blur (perf increase);60 FPS++;Increased Graphics Heap Sizes;Model LOD -2 (Highest)}"

# chocolate — EXPERIMENTAL 60 FPS test profile (added 2026-07-18, user request).
# Origin: a Reddit shadPS4 tuning post, adapted. THAT POST WAS A DESKTOP PC (RX 6800,
# 1080p, Windows) on shadPS4 0.13/0.14; we pin 0.16.0 on a Deck. Deliberately DROPPED as
# inapplicable: "select your strongest GPU" (Deck has one APU), "AMD Control Center" /
# Enhanced Vsync (Windows-only), "Devkit for 1440p" (panel is 1280x800 — dev_kit_mode
# stays unset). One recommendation is INVERTED: the post disables FSR because an RX 6800
# doesn't need it; the Deck does, so FSR stays ON here.
#
# ⚠ PIVOTED TO 30 FPS 2026-07-18. Chocolate originally targeted 60 FPS; on-device it sat
#   around 45 FPS with heavy slowdown, and the user's read was that chasing 60 on a Deck
#   was itself the problem. So chocolate now runs "30 FPS++" and keeps every other patch
#   and setting unchanged. Everything else about the profile is a CONSTANT across that
#   change, which is what makes the two comparable.
#
#   RECOVERY — the exact 60 FPS set this replaced (no git yet, so it lives here):
#     1280x800 Light Grid For SteamDeck (READ NOTES);Resolution Patch 1280x800 (16:10);
#     FMOD Crash Fix;Unlock Game Region;Disable HTTP Requests;Skip Intro;
#     Disable Chromatic Aberration;Disable Motion Blur (perf increase);60 FPS++;
#     Increased Graphics Heap Sizes;Model LOD 1 (Lower)
#   Verified on-device: all 11 applied, memory_patcher write counts matched the XML
#   exactly (60 FPS++ = 192 writes). To go back, swap "30 FPS++" for "60 FPS++" below.
#
# ⚠ 30 FPS++ and 60 FPS++ share 97 ADDRESSES — mutually exclusive, NEVER both. Last-applied
#   wins and the order is the XML's, not ours, so enabling both makes the frame rate a
#   coin flip. Verified 2026-07-18: 30 FPS++ is clean against all ten other members.
# ⚠ VERIFIED 2026-07-18 against the live upstream XML (58 patches, all AppVer 01.09):
#   these 10 share NO address with each other — the set is fully additive. Re-run that
#   diff before adding anything (see the Performance Patch note above for how).
# ⚠ "Performance Patch" STAYS EXCLUDED: it clashes with FOUR of this set — 1280x800
#   Light Grid (2 addrs), 60 FPS++ (5), 30 FPS++ (5), Model LOD -2 (1).
# ⚠ "Disable vsync" is NOT a patch — no such entry exists upstream. That request is the
#   PRESENT_MODE_CHOCOLATE=Immediate setting below.
# ⚠ "Vertex Explosion fix" is NOT a patch either — it is a Nexus file-overlay MOD, so it
#   belongs to stage 40 (parked). See the 60 FPS++ tradeoff note below for why it matters.
#
# ⚠ "Model LOD 1 (Lower)" added 2026-07-18 as the first lever against a measured 45 FPS.
#   NOTE THE DIRECTION: the source Reddit post ran "Model LOD -2 (Highest)" — the
#   EXPENSIVE end — because an RX 6800 has headroom to spend. On a Deck that inverts, so
#   we go LOWER, not higher. "Model LOD 2 (Lowest)" is the next step down if LOD 1 isn't
#   enough; they are alternatives, never both (each is 1 write to the same knob).
#   Verified additive against the other 10 (no shared address).
#
# ⚠⚠ RESOLVED 2026-07-19 — the no-frame-rate-patch diagnostic came back CLEAN. ⚠⚠
#   The hypothesis was that the on-device artifacting was VERTEX EXPLOSION, i.e. a
#   deltatime artifact of the FPS++ patch family, rather than a model-detail problem.
#   Dropping "30 FPS++" made the artifacting GO AWAY, with "Model LOD 1 (Lower)" still
#   enabled throughout. Two conclusions, both single-variable and both solid:
#     - "30 FPS++" (the FPS++ family) IS the cause.
#     - "Model LOD 1 (Lower)" is NOT. It was present in the artifacting AND the clean
#       state, so the follow-up run once reserved for it is unnecessary.
#   The suspects queued behind it were never reached and are moot: GPU.fsr_enabled=true,
#   extra_dmem_in_mbytes=4000, "Increased Graphics Heap Sizes" all ran throughout the
#   clean session, so none of them artifact on their own.
#   That clean config was PROMOTED TO VANILLA (see PATCHES_VANILLA above) and ships.
#
# ⚠⚠ CHOCOLATE'S NEW MISSION 2026-07-19 — "30 FPS++" IS BACK, ON PURPOSE. ⚠⚠
#   Chocolate is no longer a subset of vanilla; it is vanilla PLUS the one patch we know
#   breaks rendering. That is the point. The open question is now:
#       does the user-supplied Nexus vertex-explosion MOD make FPS++ usable?
#   The user has downloaded that mod; this profile is the rig to test it against. Chain to
#   validate on-device, in this order:
#     1. chocolate WITHOUT the mod  -> artifacting MUST return. If it does not, the
#        2026-07-19 result was not what we think it was and everything above is suspect.
#     2. chocolate WITH the mod     -> artifacting gone => the deckborne (60 FPS + mods)
#        experience is viable, gated on the user supplying that mod.
#   ⚠ NOT YET VERIFIED, and deckborne is blocked until it is. The mod is also not yet in
#   config/mods.catalog — it needs a name/URL there before anything can gate on it.
#   ⚠ WATCH THE LOCALE TRAP (see CLAUDE.md / stage 40): this dump is EU GOTY and reads
#   menu/enggb; a mod shipped for menu/engus applies perfectly and changes NOTHING. A
#   "mod didn't help" result is only trustworthy after confirming the mod actually loaded.
#   ⚠ THIS IS NOT A SHIPPING CONFIG. Without the mod present it artifacts by design.
#   REMOVE: drop `30 FPS++;` to return chocolate to the clean set that became vanilla.
#   (The 60 FPS variant is in the RECOVERY block above if you go the other way.)
PATCHES_CHOCOLATE="${PATCHES_CHOCOLATE:-1280x800 Light Grid For SteamDeck (READ NOTES);Resolution Patch 1280x800 (16:10);FMOD Crash Fix;Unlock Game Region;Disable HTTP Requests;Skip Intro;Disable Chromatic Aberration;Disable Motion Blur (perf increase);30 FPS++;Increased Graphics Heap Sizes;Model LOD 1 (Lower)}"

# --- vblank (MUST match the frame-rate patch above) --------------------------
# ⚠ `GPU.vblankFrequency` is a FREQUENCY IN HZ. It is NOT the old "Vblank Divider".
# Verified in shadPS4 source: `static ConfigEntry<u32> vblankFrequency(60);` and
# `if (vblankFrequency.get() < 60) { vblankFrequency = 60; }` — anything below 60 is
# silently clamped UP to 60. Effective FPS = vblankFrequency / (flip_rate + 1), where
# flip_rate is what the game asks for; Bloodborne stock asks for a divide-by-2, so
# 60 Hz -> 30 FPS.
#
# HISTORY (do not re-break): this used to be written as `vblankFrequency=4`, carried
# over from divider-era guides where "divider 4" meant >60 FPS. In 0.16 that is read as
# 4 Hz and clamped to 60 — accidentally correct for 30 FPS, and silently WRONG for any
# high-FPS profile. Old divider N maps to roughly 60*N Hz (so "divider 4" == 240 Hz).
#
# PAIRING RULE, from the patch authors' own notes in Bloodborne.xml:
#   30 FPS++ / 60 FPS++ -> 60   (neither note asks for a vblank change; 60 covers both)
#   90 FPS++            -> 90+  ("Remember to change Vblank to 90 or higher.")
#   Uncap FPS++         -> higher still ("Remember to increase vblank frequency.")
# Community guidance is blunt about the cost of getting this wrong: "the majority of
# issues are caused by improper VBLANK settings".
# NB the real floor is 30, not 60, and it is READ-SIDE ONLY: a written value below 30
# persists in the file while every consumer sees 30. So the file can lie about this key.
VBLANK_HZ_VANILLA="${VBLANK_HZ_VANILLA:-60}"
VBLANK_HZ_DECKBORNE="${VBLANK_HZ_DECKBORNE:-60}"   # bump ONLY alongside a >60 FPS patch
# chocolate runs 30 FPS++ (restored 2026-07-19), and the pairing rule above is explicit
# that 30/60 FPS++ both want 60. Only 90 FPS++ / Uncap ask for more. So this stays 60 — do
# NOT "helpfully" raise it to match a frame target; 60 Hz is correct for both ++ variants.
VBLANK_HZ_CHOCOLATE="${VBLANK_HZ_CHOCOLATE:-60}"

# --- emulator settings written to config.json --------------------------------
# OFF — tried, measured, and it never worked. Leaving this documented so nobody
# "discovers" it again.
#
# The theory was sound: shadPS4 recompiles everything each launch (measured: 208 graphics
# pipelines + 312 shaders + 35 compute in one session, twice logged as "Stall during
# rendering"), and this setting is supposed to persist them. It writes fine — the log ends
# with `cache_storage.cpp:136 Close: Cache dumped`. It just never LOADS.
#
# Every single run logs:
#   vk_pipeline_serialization.cpp:318 WarmUp: Pipeline cache isn't compatible with
#   current system. Ignoring the cache
# Three consecutive runs on identical hardware, with the cache deleted beforehand so run 1
# wrote a fresh profile from the real device — run 2 still rejected it.
#
# SUSPECTED UPSTREAM BUG (strong, not proven): the check is
#   memcmp(profile_data.data(), &profile, sizeof(profile))
# over Shader::Profile — 3×u64, 4×u32 and 36×bool, so the struct carries significant
# PADDING. memcmp compares padding bytes, and aggregate initialisation does not guarantee
# they are zeroed, so two runs can differ byte-wise while every capability value matches.
# No member varies at runtime (no pointers/timestamps/counters), which rules out a
# legitimate mismatch. NOT verified: the construction site in vk_pipeline_cache.cpp.
#
# Cost of leaving it on: hundreds of files written per launch, never read, with NO size
# limit and NO eviction anywhere in cache_storage.cpp — unbounded growth on a handheld.
# So it goes back to upstream's default. Re-test after a shadPS4 update; if a run ever
# logs "Preloaded N pipelines", it started working.
#
# NB an earlier theory blamed OUR warm-up (headless gamescope writing a profile no real
# launch could match). That was WRONG — deleting the cache didn't help. The
# discard_warmup_shader_cache() cleanup in 50_steam_shortcut.sh stays anyway: it's
# correct hygiene and costs nothing.
PIPELINE_CACHE="${PIPELINE_CACHE:-false}"

# Deck's native panel. shadPS4 defaults to 1280x720, and the Light Grid patch we enable
# is explicitly keyed to the WINDOW/FULLSCREEN resolution (1280x800 variant).
WINDOW_W="${WINDOW_W:-1280}"
WINDOW_H="${WINDOW_H:-800}"

# shadPS4 has SEPARATE internal (render) resolution keys, defaulting to 1280x720. Since
# stage 35 enables "Resolution Patch 1280x800 (16:10)", which makes the GAME render at
# 1280x800, leaving the emulator's internal screen at 720 is an obvious mismatch.
# ⚠ COUPLED-BUT-UNVERIFIED: the patch and these keys are set together on the assumption
# they should agree, but the exact interaction between the game's patched render size and
# shadPS4's internal_screen_* was NOT traced in source. If the image looks letterboxed,
# stretched, or the UI sits wrong, REVERT THESE FIRST (back to 1280/720) before blaming
# the resolution patch — they are the less-verified half of the pair.
INTERNAL_W="${INTERNAL_W:-1280}"
INTERNAL_H="${INTERNAL_H:-800}"

# The desktop target's window/internal resolution. Used ONLY by DECKBORNE_TARGET=desktop;
# every profile and every other target keeps the Deck panel values above.
# Set together with the `Optimal 1080p` + `1080p Light Grid` pair in the patch string — the
# light-grid patch keys off the WINDOW resolution and the render patch off the INTERNAL one,
# so changing either patch without these (or the reverse) mismatches them.
WINDOW_W_DESKTOP="${WINDOW_W_DESKTOP:-1920}"
WINDOW_H_DESKTOP="${WINDOW_H_DESKTOP:-1080}"
INTERNAL_W_DESKTOP="${INTERNAL_W_DESKTOP:-1920}"
INTERNAL_H_DESKTOP="${INTERNAL_H_DESKTOP:-1080}"

# Swapchain presentation. Valid values are EXACTLY "Mailbox", "Fifo", "Immediate" —
# case-sensitive. An unrecognised string falls back to Mailbox; a mode the driver doesn't
# advertise falls back to Fifo. Neither writes back to the file.
# ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-07-18: this used to say both fallbacks happen "SILENTLY". They do NOT —
# both are logged by vk_swapchain.cpp:192 FindPresentMode():
#     unknown string  -> LOG_ERROR   "Unknown present mode {}, defaulting to Mailbox."
#     not advertised  -> LOG_WARNING "Requested present mode {} is not supported, falling
#                                     back to {}."
# That matters: config.json is NOT evidence of the mode in use — shad_log.txt is. Believing
# the "silent" claim is what let chocolate run four sessions on Fifo while its config said
# Immediate. ALWAYS confirm the effective mode from the log.
# "Mailbox" (triple-buffered, non-blocking, no tearing) is both the default and the
# right choice; confirmed on-device that no fallback warning was logged. "Immediate" is
# the only lower-latency option and it tears.
PRESENT_MODE="${PRESENT_MODE:-Mailbox}"

# --- chocolate profile: emulator settings that DIFFER from the above ---------
# These apply ONLY to DECKBORNE_PROFILE=chocolate. vanilla/deckborne are untouched, and
# stage 30 writes the extra keys for chocolate only — so a bad experiment here cannot
# change the shipping profiles.
#
# ⚠ ALL FOUR KEY NAMES VERIFIED 2026-07-18 against shadPS4 v.0.16.0
#   src/core/emulator_settings.h (the NLOHMANN_DEFINE_TYPE_NON_INTRUSIVE member lists —
#   those ARE the JSON keys). Do not trust a GUI label or a guide for these.
#
# ⚠ WAS "Immediate" (the post's "Disable vsync" request). CHANGED TO Fifo 2026-07-18 after
# the Deck proved Immediate IS NOT AVAILABLE HERE. Every one of the four chocolate runs
# logged, at vk_swapchain.cpp:219:
#     FindPresentMode: Requested present mode Immediate is not supported, falling back to Fifo.
# The Deck's Vulkan driver does not advertise IMMEDIATE for this surface, so the emulator
# fell back to Fifo — meaning chocolate has been running Fifo since its first sync and
# every perf observation so far was made under Fifo, not Immediate. Setting it explicitly
# changes NO behaviour; it stops config.json claiming something the driver never honoured.
#
# ⚠ CONSEQUENCE: "disable vsync" is NOT ACHIEVABLE on this Deck via present_mode. Fifo and
#   Mailbox both sync. Don't re-try Immediate expecting tearing-for-latency; you get Fifo.
#
# ⚠ AND IT LIKELY EXPLAINS THE "45 FPS WITH SLOWDOWN" REPORT. Under Fifo, presentation is
#   QUANTIZED to the refresh: at vblank 60 you can present at 60, 30, 20, 15 — nothing in
#   between. A game rendering ~45fps worth of work therefore alternates 60/30/60/30, which
#   averages to a ~45 counter reading while FEELING like constant judder. That matches the
#   report exactly. With 30 FPS++ now in play, if the Deck renders 30 comfortably Fifo
#   paces it as a clean locked 30 with no alternation — which should feel far better than
#   "45" suggests. THIS IS THE THING TO CONFIRM ON THE NEXT RUN.
#
# The three valid values are EXACTLY Mailbox|Fifo|Immediate, case-sensitive — verified in
# v.0.16.0 vk_swapchain.cpp:192 FindPresentMode(). Vulkan's fourth standard mode,
# FIFO_RELAXED, is NOT exposed by shadPS4 (zero references in the settings or swapchain
# source). Fifo = classic vsync, never tears, hard framerate steps, and the only mode the
# Vulkan spec requires drivers to support. Mailbox = triple buffering, no tearing, lower
# latency, but renders frames it discards (heat/battery on a handheld).
PRESENT_MODE_CHOCOLATE="${PRESENT_MODE_CHOCOLATE:-Fifo}"
# vanilla inherits Fifo from the chocolate promotion (2026-07-19). ⚠ THIS IS A REAL CHANGE,
# not a copy of an existing value: vanilla used to take PRESENT_MODE=Mailbox (the base
# default, further up). Every clean on-device observation behind the promotion was made
# under Fifo, so shipping vanilla on Mailbox would ship a config that was never tested.
# deckborne still takes the Mailbox base default — it is frozen and untouched by this.
PRESENT_MODE_VANILLA="${PRESENT_MODE_VANILLA:-Fifo}"
# deckborne joins them on Fifo with the 2026-07-19 promotion. ⚠ REAL CHANGE: deckborne took
# the Mailbox base default until now, and every clean on-device observation behind the
# promotion was made under Fifo — shipping it on Mailbox would ship a config never tested.
PRESENT_MODE_DECKBORNE="${PRESENT_MODE_DECKBORNE:-Fifo}"
DECKBORNE_PRESENT_MODE="${DECKBORNE_PRESENT_MODE:-auto}"
#
# ⚠ RE-TESTED 2026-07-18 ON CHOCOLATE AND IT STILL DOES NOT WORK. Back to false.
# This was the CLEANEST test the project has run, and it is why the question is settled:
#   run 1 (20:02) — no profile.bin existed ("No such file"), so nothing to reject; the
#                   run ended "Cache dumped", writing a fresh profile from THIS device.
#   run 2 (20:22) — that profile.bin WAS read, and rejected:
#                   vk_pipeline_serialization.cpp:318 WarmUp: Pipeline cache isn't
#                   compatible with current system. Ignoring the cache
# Same device, same build, 20 minutes apart, nothing changed in between — which rules out
# a stale profile, a config difference, or a hardware change as the explanation.
# The compile counts confirm it saved NOTHING: 291 shaders/187 gfx pipelines on run 1 vs
# 275/174 on run 2 (the delta is a different route through the level, not cache reuse).
# That is now FOUR consecutive failures and it strengthens the padding-in-memcmp theory
# documented in the PIPELINE_CACHE block above.
# Cost of leaving it on is NOT neutral — cache_storage.cpp has no size limit and no
# eviction, so it writes hundreds of files per launch, forever, on a handheld, and never
# reads them. Hence false.
# Re-test only after a shadPS4 UPDATE, and only trust a log line reading "Preloaded N
# pipelines". Absence of the warning is NOT success — run 1 had no warning purely because
# there was no file yet.
# NB still never tested: Vulkan.pipeline_cache_archived (it was false throughout).
PIPELINE_CACHE_CHOCOLATE="${PIPELINE_CACHE_CHOCOLATE:-false}"
DECKBORNE_SHADER_CACHE="${DECKBORNE_SHADER_CACHE:-auto}"
#
# ⚠ "Log type: Async" DOES NOT EXIST ON LINUX. LogSettings::type is inside `#ifdef _WIN32`
# (default "wincolor") and is NOT in the non-Windows NLOHMANN key list — writing Log.type
# here would be a key the emulator never reads, which is precisely the class of bug that
# made this project's config.toml a no-op for weeks. The portable equivalent is the `sync`
# bool, which IS serialised on both platforms: async == sync:false. Default is true.
LOG_SYNC_CHOCOLATE="${LOG_SYNC_CHOCOLATE:-false}"
LOG_SYNC_VANILLA="${LOG_SYNC_VANILLA:-false}"
LOG_SYNC_DECKBORNE="${LOG_SYNC_DECKBORNE:-false}"
#
# Log.append — ON for every profile as of 2026-07-25, and this is a DIAGNOSTIC fix, not a
# tuning choice: it changes nothing about how the game runs.
#
# ⚠ THE PROBLEM IT SOLVES. shadPS4 TRUNCATES shad_log.txt on every launch (upstream default
# `append{false}`), so the file only ever holds the MOST RECENT launch. Every DeckBorne
# install ends by launching the game for ~15s (the Recent Games warm-up in stage 50), so
# installing a second profile DESTROYS the first one's log before anyone can collect it.
# Verified 2026-07-25 across eight captured snapshots: every single one contained exactly
# one `Run: Starting shadps4 emulator` line. Three profile switches were tested on-device
# that day and only the last one left any emulator-side evidence.
#
# That matters because shad_log.txt is the ONLY place this project trusts for what actually
# applied — "where truth lives" in CLAUDE.md. Losing it turns a profile-switch test into an
# anecdote.
#
# ⚠ Growth is bounded by shadPS4's own LogSettings.size_limit (default 100MB), which we do
# not change — so this cannot fill a Deck.
# ⚠ Consequence for `collect`: state-*/shad_log.txt now carries the whole session rather
# than one launch, so snapshots get bigger and each launch must be located by its
# `Run: Starting shadps4 emulator` marker instead of assuming the file is one run.
LOG_APPEND="${LOG_APPEND:-true}"
#
# ⚠⚠ AND THE CONFIG KEY ALONE DOES NOT WORK. Log.append is written, persisted and
# round-tripped by the emulator — and the log is STILL truncated every launch. Proven
# on-device 2026-07-25: nine snapshots all had `"append": true` in the config the emulator
# itself wrote back, and every one still held a single launch, with a later log SMALLER than
# an earlier one and not a byte-prefix of it.
#
# THE CAUSE IS AN ORDERING BUG UPSTREAM (src/main.cpp at v.0.16.0, revision 5be3f0a3 — the
# exact commit the shipped AppImage reports):
#     line 107   Common::Log::Setup("shad_log.txt")   <- config NOT loaded yet, so
#                                                        g_should_append is still false
#                                                        => the file is TRUNCATED here
#     line 124   Common::Log::Shutdown()
#     line 126   g_should_append |= EmulatorSettings.IsLogAppend()   <- too late
#     line 127   Common::Log::Setup("shad_log.txt")   <- reopened in append mode, but the
#                                                        file was already emptied at 107
# The CLI flag is bound straight to the global (`app.add_flag("--log-append",
# Common::Log::g_should_append)`) and argv is parsed at line 98 — BEFORE the first Setup. So
# the flag lands in time and the config key never can.
#
# Hence the flag below, appended to the Steam tile's LaunchOptions by stage 50. LOG_APPEND
# above is kept as well: harmless, correct in intent, and it starts working for free if
# upstream ever moves the first Setup after the config load.
# ⚠ Set this empty to drop the flag. ⚠ Changing it CHANGES LaunchOptions, so stage 50's
# skip check (--expect-launch-options) will rebuild an existing tile once. That is correct,
# not a bug — the tile has to carry the new flag.
SHADPS4_LOG_APPEND_FLAG="${SHADPS4_LOG_APPEND_FLAG:---log-append}"
#
# General.extra_dmem_in_mbytes — int, shadPS4 default 0. Writing it at all is the change,
# not the exact number. The source post used 6000 on a desktop; these are deliberately
# conservative on a 16GB SHARED-memory Deck (system + GPU come out of the same pool).
# ⚠ FIRST SUSPECT if either profile OOMs, crashes, or stutters worse than deckborne. It is
# a clean knob to bisect: 4000 -> 6000 -> 8000, one Deck run each.
# ⚠ THE PROFILES DIVERGED 2026-07-20: vanilla dropped to 2000, deckborne stays at 4000 on
# the reasoning that it carries mods and a frame-rate patch. ONLY 4000 HAS EVER RUN
# ON-DEVICE — the artifact-free 2026-07-19 session was at 4000 for every profile, so
# vanilla's 2000 is an untested value. If vanilla misbehaves and deckborne does not, this
# is the first thing to put back.
# The desktop target, 2026-07-25 — 6000 is the value the source Reddit post actually used on
# its RX 6800, and the "conservative on a 16GB SHARED-memory Deck" reasoning above does not
# apply to a machine with dedicated VRAM. Only DECKBORNE_TARGET=desktop reads this; the two
# Deck targets stay on EXTRA_DMEM_MB_DECKBORNE.
# ⚠ FIRST SUSPECT if the desktop target OOMs or crashes: bisect 6000 -> 4000.
EXTRA_DMEM_MB_DESKTOP="${EXTRA_DMEM_MB_DESKTOP:-6000}"
EXTRA_DMEM_MB_CHOCOLATE="${EXTRA_DMEM_MB_CHOCOLATE:-4000}"
EXTRA_DMEM_MB_VANILLA="${EXTRA_DMEM_MB_VANILLA:-2000}"
# deckborne as of the 2026-07-19 promotion. Confirmed live on-device by the emulator itself:
# `memory.cpp:63 SetupMemoryRegions: extraDmemInMbytes is 4000 MB!`
EXTRA_DMEM_MB_DECKBORNE="${EXTRA_DMEM_MB_DECKBORNE:-4000}"
#
# FSR ON — the INVERSION of the source post (it disabled FSR because an RX 6800 has
# headroom the Deck does not). Setting it explicitly makes the profile reproducible.
# Siblings we do NOT set: rcas_enabled, rcas_attenuation.
# ⚠ UPDATED 2026-07-19: this used to note that stage 30 "has never written GPU.fsr_enabled
# for any profile". Still true of DECKBORNE, which runs on whatever the emulator defaults
# to; vanilla now writes it explicitly as part of the chocolate promotion. FSR was on for
# the entire clean on-device session, so it is not an artifacting suspect.
FSR_CHOCOLATE="${FSR_CHOCOLATE:-true}"
FSR_VANILLA="${FSR_VANILLA:-true}"
FSR_DECKBORNE="${FSR_DECKBORNE:-true}"
# ⚠ THE DESKTOP TARGET INVERTS IT BACK, 2026-07-25. FSR is ON everywhere else because the Deck
# needs upscaling; the source post DISABLED it on an RX 6800 for exactly the opposite reason.
# The desktop target renders at native 1080p, so upscaling is cost with nothing to buy.
# Siblings still unset: rcas_enabled (default true), rcas_attenuation (250) — they only do
# anything while FSR is on, so with FSR off here they are moot.
FSR_DESKTOP="${FSR_DESKTOP:-false}"

# HDR output — PER-PROFILE as of 2026-07-25.
#   vanilla              — false. Vanilla means the game as it shipped, and Bloodborne shipped
#                          in 2015 without HDR. Permitting an output mode the original never
#                          had is exactly the kind of "small, reasonable" addition that makes
#                          vanilla stop meaning anything. Same reasoning that keeps the FPS
#                          counter and the frame-rate patches out of it.
#   deckborne/chocolate  — true. These are the tuned lanes, and a user running a mod that adds
#                          HDR output should not have to discover an emulator setting to make
#                          it work.
# ⚠ EVERY PROFILE MUST WRITE THIS KEY, whatever its value. config.json is merged, so a key some
# profiles write and others omit LEAKS across a switch instead of reverting — a user going
# deckborne -> vanilla would keep HDR permitted. That is why there is no "" (don't write) option
# here, unlike extra_dmem. See the trap in CLAUDE.md.
# ⚠ The Steam Deck OLED *is* HDR-capable, so this is not automatically inert on a Deck the way
# the other desktop-only settings are — but see the Bloodborne note below, which makes it moot
# in practice for the stock game on any hardware.
# ⚠ IT IS A PERMISSION, NOT A COMMAND, and that is why turning it on is safe. Verified in
# vk_swapchain.cpp v0.16.0:
#     supports_hdr = <HDR10 ST2084 / Rec.2020 PQ present in the driver's surface formats>;
#     supports_hdr &= EmulatorSettings.IsHdrAllowed();
# So it is ANDed with a real capability query. On a display or driver that does not advertise
# Rec.2020 PQ the flag is simply ignored and the normal SDR swapchain is used — there is no
# failure mode where enabling this breaks a non-HDR monitor.
# ⚠ IT IS ALSO INERT FOR STOCK BLOODBORNE. The game is a 2015 title, predating PS4 HDR, so it
# never asks the emulator for HDR output and Swapchain::SetHDR is never called. This is set for
# the case where a MOD adds HDR output: the emulator side is then already permitted, instead of
# the user having to discover this key. Reason it is on, not evidence that it does anything.
# ⚠ YOU CANNOT CONFIRM IT FROM A NORMAL LOG. Unlike present_mode (which logs a warning when the
# driver refuses), HDR only appears at LOG_DEBUG ("Recreate the swapchain: ... HDR={}"). Absence
# of that line is not evidence either way at default verbosity.
HDR_VANILLA="${HDR_VANILLA:-false}"
HDR_DECKBORNE="${HDR_DECKBORNE:-true}"
HDR_CHOCOLATE="${HDR_CHOCOLATE:-true}"
# Fallback for a profile added later that does not wire itself into the case in stage 30.
# ⚠ NOT the value the three profiles above use — setting this alone changes nothing for them.
HDR_ALLOWED="${HDR_ALLOWED:-false}"
DECKBORNE_HDR="${DECKBORNE_HDR:-auto}"

# Which GPU the desktop target renders on. This is the source post's "select your strongest
# GPU" — meaningless on a Deck (one APU), potentially significant on a desktop with an iGPU
# alongside a discrete card.
#
# ⚠⚠ -1 (AUTO) IS THE RIGHT ANSWER AND DECKBORNE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT OVERRIDE IT. Read
# vk_instance.cpp v0.16.0 before "improving" this: when gpu_id < 0 shadPS4 ALREADY does the
# selection itself, sorting every physical device by (1) supports Vulkan 1.3, (2) is a
# DISCRETE_GPU, (3) largest device-local memory heap, and taking the winner. That IS "pick the
# strongest GPU", decided from each device's real Vulkan properties — strictly better than
# anything we could compute from outside the emulator. The post's advice predates this
# (shadPS4 0.13/0.14, where its Qt GUI made you choose).
#
# ⚠ A NON-NEGATIVE VALUE IS DANGEROUS. It bypasses that ranking entirely and indexes raw
# vkEnumeratePhysicalDevices order, and an out-of-range index hits
#   ASSERT_MSG(physical_device_index < num_physical_devices, ...)
# which calls assert_fail_impl() — it is NOT compiled out in release, so a wrong number is a
# hard startup failure, not a fallback.
#
# So this stays -1, written EXPLICITLY rather than left absent: a user who experimented with
# a GPU index in the emulator's own settings gets reset to auto, which keeps the target
# reproducible. Override it only to force a device that is NOT the best one (e.g. deliberately
# running on the iGPU). scripts/detect_gpu.py prints the device list and the index shadPS4
# will land on; stage 30 runs it for this target so the run log carries it.
VULKAN_GPU_ID="${VULKAN_GPU_ID:--1}"
VULKAN_GPU_ID_DESKTOP="${VULKAN_GPU_ID_DESKTOP:-$VULKAN_GPU_ID}"

# Only the exact string "Fullscreen" gets an exclusive display mode (sdl_window.cpp);
# every other value — including "Fullscreen (Borderless)" and any typo — passes NULL,
# which SDL3 treats as borderless-fullscreen-desktop. `full_screen` is the on/off switch
# and is INDEPENDENT of the mode; both must be set.
FULLSCREEN_MODE="${FULLSCREEN_MODE:-Fullscreen}"

# On-screen FPS counter — NOW PER-PROFILE (2026-07-23). It used to be one global that all
# three profiles shared; the block above it said "decide at DISTRIBUTION time whether a
# shipped build should default it off", and that decision is now made: OFF for the two
# SHIPPING profiles, ON for the dev lane.
#   vanilla / deckborne — false. These are what a user installs to PLAY; a permanent
#     counter over the corner of Bloodborne is a debug overlay, not a feature.
#   chocolate — true. It is the measuring rig, and the counter is the only instrument this
#     project has for judging a change. Four rounds were spent arguing about whether
#     something "felt" smoother before it existed; it is what turned the resolution-patch
#     win from a vibe into "locked 30". Turning it off here would cost real diagnostic
#     ability, so it stays.
# ⚠ CONSEQUENCE FOR TESTING: the two profiles a user actually runs now have NO on-screen
#   frame instrument. If someone reports "it feels worse", the counter is not there to
#   check against — re-run the comparison on chocolate, or flip the var for one run:
#     SHOW_FPS_DECKBORNE=true ./install.sh 30
#   Each is independently env-overridable, exactly like EXTRA_DMEM_MB_* and FSR_*.
SHOW_FPS_VANILLA="${SHOW_FPS_VANILLA:-false}"
SHOW_FPS_DECKBORNE="${SHOW_FPS_DECKBORNE:-false}"
SHOW_FPS_CHOCOLATE="${SHOW_FPS_CHOCOLATE:-true}"
# Fallback for any profile added later that does not wire itself into the case in stage 30.
# ⚠ It is NOT the value the three profiles above use — setting DECKBORNE_SHOW_FPS alone
# changes nothing for them. Use the per-profile var.
DECKBORNE_SHOW_FPS="${DECKBORNE_SHOW_FPS:-true}"
DECKBORNE_FPS_COUNTER="${DECKBORNE_FPS_COUNTER:-auto}"

# --- Steam launcher tile ----------------------------------------------------
# Overridable from the environment so a tile can be registered under a throwaway
# name without editing this file, e.g.
#   STEAM_TILE_NAME="Bloodborne Test" ./install.sh 50
# The name feeds the grid appid (see steam/add_shortcut.py), so a new name means
# an appid Steam has never seen — which is how we test first-run tile behaviour.
STEAM_TILE_NAME="${STEAM_TILE_NAME:-Bloodborne}"
STEAM_TILE_ARTWORK_DIR="${STEAM_TILE_ARTWORK_DIR:-payloads/artwork}"  # grid/hero/logo/icon images (optional)

# --- Recent Games warm-up ---------------------------------------------------
# Steam only lists a game in Recent once it has actually launched it (see CLAUDE.md
# "Steam facts"), so 50_steam_shortcut.sh boots the tile once and stops it again.
#
# DECKBORNE_WARMUP=0 skips the warm-up entirely. Reach for it if a run ever leaves the
# game stuck on screen — the tile then lands in Recent after the first manual launch
# instead, which costs nothing but a launch.
DECKBORNE_WARMUP="${DECKBORNE_WARMUP:-1}"
DECKBORNE_WARMUP_SETTLE="${DECKBORNE_WARMUP_SETTLE:-20}"  # after Steam's process is up, before launching: up != shortcuts loaded
DECKBORNE_WARMUP_DWELL="${DECKBORNE_WARMUP_DWELL:-15}"    # how long the game runs so Steam records the session
DECKBORNE_WARMUP_GRACE="${DECKBORNE_WARMUP_GRACE:-15}"    # seconds to wait for a TERMed game to exit before KILL
# Run the warm-up launch under a HEADLESS gamescope so it can never own the screen —
# insurance for the one place DeckBorne launches the game itself (the warm-up bug's
# home). The tile is written with headless launch options for the warm-up only, then
# restored to plain visible options for real play. Falls back to a visible (still
# stop_warmup-safe) launch where gamescope can't go headless — e.g. the aarch64 dev
# box. Set 0 to force the plain visible warm-up.
DECKBORNE_WARMUP_HEADLESS="${DECKBORNE_WARMUP_HEADLESS:-1}"
# After the uninstall restarts Steam, how long to let the client root itself in its
# systemd scope before confirming it stayed up. Exiting too fast races that setup and
# leaves Steam down (the launcher flickers, then dies). Bump if 10s proves too short.
DECKBORNE_STEAM_SETTLE="${DECKBORNE_STEAM_SETTLE:-10}"
# Log-only probes in 50_steam_shortcut.sh. DECKBORNE_PROBE=1 prints what the warm-up
# kill would target; DECKBORNE_PROBE_CMD=1 additionally wraps LaunchOptions to test
# Steam's %command% expansion — that one pollutes the tile, so use a throwaway
# STEAM_TILE_NAME with it.
DECKBORNE_PROBE="${DECKBORNE_PROBE:-1}"
DECKBORNE_PROBE_CMD="${DECKBORNE_PROBE_CMD:-0}"
